  <section id="chto-eto">
    <h2>What it is</h2>
    <p>The carotid arteries run up the neck and carry blood to the brain. With age, cholesterol and calcium build up on their walls, forming <strong>atherosclerotic plaques</strong>. Most often they sit where the common carotid artery divides into the internal and external branches.</p>
    <p>A plaque is dangerous in two ways. It narrows the lumen, like scale in a pipe, and the brain gets less blood. More importantly, a fragment may break off or a clot may form on it: it travels with the blood to the brain and blocks a vessel. This is how an ischaemic stroke happens.</p>
    <h3>Risk factors</h3>
    <p>Age over 50, high blood pressure, high cholesterol, smoking, diabetes, a sedentary lifestyle, excess weight.</p>
  </section>

  <section id="simptomy">
    <h2>Symptoms</h2>
    <p>Most often — <strong>none</strong>. A plaque does not hurt, and people live with it for years without knowing. Some are lucky: it is found by chance on an ultrasound and removed in time. In others the first sign is a <strong>stroke</strong> — when a fragment breaks off. So if you have risk factors, it is worth having a neck ultrasound without waiting for symptoms.</p>
    <p>Sometimes a plaque shows itself with a <strong>transient ischaemic attack</strong> — brief symptoms that pass on their own within minutes or hours:</p>
    <ul>
      <li>weakness or numbness of an arm, leg or half of the face;</li>
      <li>speech problems;</li>
      <li>sudden loss of vision in one eye, a "curtain" in front of the eye.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>Even if everything has passed, this signals a high risk of stroke in the near future — urgent investigation is needed.</p>
  </section>

  <section id="diagnostika">
    <h2>Diagnosis</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Carotid duplex ultrasound</strong> — the first test. It shows the plaque, the degree of narrowing and the plaque structure.</li>
      <li><strong>CT angiography</strong> — the second key test: it shows the plaque and the brain vessels precisely. If contraindicated, MR angiography is done. They clarify the degree of narrowing, the length of the plaque and the state of the brain vessels before surgery.</li>
      <li><strong>Brain MRI</strong> — if there were symptoms: it shows whether there have been silent strokes.</li>
    </ul>
    <div class="callout">
      <p><strong>The percentage in your report is not a verdict.</strong> Narrowing is measured on two scales: the European <strong>ECST</strong> and the North American <strong>NASCET</strong>. They differ considerably, and the European scale often gives higher numbers. So the same plaque may "weigh" differently in different reports — for example 80% in one and noticeably less in another.</p>
      <p>Relying on the number alone is not a good idea. A specialist should measure the narrowing on the images and, considering symptoms and plaque structure, decide whether surgery is needed. I talk about this <a href={`${tg}/195`} target="_blank" rel="noopener">in a video on my Telegram channel</a> (in Russian).</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section id="lechenie">
    <h2>Treatment</h2>
    <p><strong>Everyone with plaques</strong> needs medication — drugs that lower cholesterol and prevent clots, chosen by a doctor — and lifestyle changes: stopping smoking, controlling blood pressure, sugar and weight, physical activity.</p>
    <p><strong>Surgery</strong> is usually considered if:</p>
    <ul>
      <li>the narrowing is 60% or more, even without symptoms;</li>
      <li>the narrowing is 50% or more and there has already been a stroke or transient ischaemic attack;</li>
      <li>the plaque is unstable — soft, with signs of a risk of breaking up.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>The final decision is individual, taking into account age, other illnesses and the state of the other artery.</p>
  </section>

  <section id="operaciya">
    <h2>Surgery</h2>
    <h3>Carotid endarterectomy</h3>
    <p>Through an incision in the neck the artery is exposed, temporarily clamped, opened and the whole plaque removed, after which the vessel is closed. Blood flow through the artery is restored. The operation takes about an hour and a half on average. It can be done under local anaesthesia, but in most cases I operate under general anaesthesia.</p>
    <p><strong>If there are no contraindications, carotid endarterectomy is the procedure of choice</strong> according to Russian and international guidelines. Stenting is an alternative for selected cases.</p>
    <h3>Stenting</h3>
    <p>Through a puncture of the artery in the groin a catheter is passed to the narrowing and a stent — a mesh tube that widens the lumen — is placed. This option is chosen when open surgery is difficult: the plaque is high, the neck has had previous surgery or radiotherapy.</p>
